WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.230
The Mother of Dragons got the mother of all
surprises on the last-ever episode of Game

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.430
of Thrones, and the actress who plays her
has got something to say about the show's

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:09.519
final, heartbreaking twist.

00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:12.179
"Best season ever!"

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:13.869
Okay, no - not that.

00:00:13.869 --> 00:00:15.400
Anyway, spoilers from here on out.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.330
"I'll tell you everything."

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.260
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,
Daenerys Targaryen star Emilia Clarke broke

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.259
her silence on the finale, admitting to the
public that she was in a state of total disbelief

00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:27.270
after reading the scripts for season 8.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:31.550
Clarke had just finished shooting Solo: A
Star Wars Story in October of 2017 when she

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.850
learned that the character she had played
for almost a decade would die at the hands

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.170
of her lover during the show's final episode.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:41.059
She touched down at Heathrow Airport in London
and "completely freaked out" when she received

00:00:41.059 --> 00:00:44.670
the scripts electronically - nearly leaving
her suitcases at Heathrow's baggage claim

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.690
because she was so eager to get home and find
out what would happen during the show's final

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:49.690
episode.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.219
At that point, Clarke had yet to face the
facts of Daenerys' downfall.

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:54.250
When she did, however, she was beside herself.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.719
She told Entertainment Weekly:

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:57.179
"I got myself situated.

00:00:57.179 --> 00:00:58.350
I got my cup of tea.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.329
I had to physically prepare the space and
then begin reading them."

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:05.440
Excitement turned to anguish as Clarke learned
of the events that led to Dany's devastating

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.580
death: her desperate fight against the undead,
the deaths of her closest friends and allies,

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:14.540
the loss of her second dragon and, eventually,
the destruction of King's Landing at her hand.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.720
Having lost nearly everything, Dany finally
reaches the Iron Throne - only to lose it

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.790
all in a moment.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:23.240
It took hours for Clarke to settle back into
herself after the revelation.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.240
She explained:

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.240
"I cried.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.240
And I went for a walk.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.220
I walked out of the house and took my keys
and phone and walked back with blisters on

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.220
my feet.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.490
I didn't come back for five hours.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.330
I'm like, 'How am I going to do this?

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.740
Because it comes out of f---ing nowhere."

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.580
[when you say "worst TV ending ever" but then
remember Dexter's]

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.640
Learning that Daenerys would turn dark during
the final two episodes of Game of Thrones,

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.880
then be killed by a man she truly loved, did
a number on Clarke - whose emotions ran high

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.490
as she tried to process everything.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:50.490
She said:

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.810
"I called my mom and [said] 'I read the scripts
and I don't want to tell you what happens

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.610
but can you just talk me off this ledge?

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.340
It really messed me up."

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.460
Despite being shocked out of her mind about
the events of the show's finale, Clarke noted

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.270
that the ending Dany got was the one that
made the most sense for her character.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.270
She explained:

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:08.030
"After 10 years of working on this show, it's
logical.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:09.030
Where else can she go?

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.150
I tried to think what the ending will be.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:14.799
It's not like she's suddenly going to go,
'Okay, I'm gonna put a kettle on and put cookies

00:02:14.799 --> 00:02:18.640
in the oven and we'll just sit down and have
a lovely time and pop a few kids out.'

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:19.950
That was never going to happen.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.439
She's a Targaryen."

00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:25.480
Clarke added that she felt Dany's conclusion
served as a full-circle moment for the naive

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:28.340
young woman viewers met on the first season
of Game of Thrones.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.340
She said:

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.980
"It's a very beautiful and touching ending.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.580
Hopefully, what you'll see in that last moment
as she's dying is: There's the vulnerability

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.999
- there's the little girl you met in season
1.

00:02:36.999 --> 00:02:37.999
See?

00:02:37.999 --> 00:02:38.999
She's right there.

00:02:38.999 --> 00:02:40.329
And now, she's not there anymore."

00:02:40.329 --> 00:02:44.629
Game of Thrones' final season will likely
go down in history as incredibly divisive,

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:46.560
and hugely disappointing to some.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.920
But no matter whether you loved how Game of
Thrones ended or not, there's no denying that

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:54.629
the end of the show's journey - as well as
Dany's - won't be forgotten anytime soon.

